On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:30:44 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I want to think through some other options here... I think drop --empty
> is useful so that it doesn't include the replies etc. I don't want to
> hack around the tooling with more tooling.

Agreed. I withdraw "[PATCH] libexec/mail: Apply message-only commits
as empty" [1] and have dropped it from my tree.

> 1. Don't post patches - just post the commit messages as an email. It's
> just text, nothing is getting applied. If I want to rewrite a commit
> message, I can just copy it and edit it.

I recommend this one. A plan is for review, not for applying, and the
list already handles review: you reply with your rewrite quoted under
mine, and I fold it into my commits. Nothing new is needed on either
side.

"[RFC PATCH 0/7] Write the commit rules down once" [2] already works
this way if you skip its fetch instructions. Each of 1/7 to 7/7 is one
commit message as plain text, so you can reply to any of them with
your version. For the next plan I will send a single [RFC] mail with
the messages in order, not format-patch output, so it does not look
like something to apply.

> 2. Marker files. Don't post empty commits. Just have a marker file for
> that specific commit. That might actually be useful for tooling to
> indicate where a particlar patch is in the process. Either it writes a
> specific filename that indicates the state, or it writes the state as
> the content of a fixed file name.

This would work with am.sh as it is, but it costs more than it gives
for now:

- Every marker has to be removed before the series lands. That is one
  more rewrite of every commit, and a marker that is missed lands on
  trunk.
- A fixed file name means every commit in the series edits the same
  file. Reordering, splitting, or dropping a plan commit then
  conflicts, and reshaping the plan is the point of the review. One
  file name per commit avoids that, but fills the tree with files.
- The list already records the state: [RFC PATCH] against [PATCH], the
  replies, and the notmuch tags. A marker would be a second record to
  keep in step with the first.

If a tool later needs to read a patch's state from the tree, marker
files are worth another look then. Until then I would keep the plan as
text on the list.

The open question in the plan's cover letter still stands: should the
72-column check skip indented lines, such as quoted code or a long URL?
I recommend skipping them.

